package brenn:processor@0.3.0;

/// The `blobs` interface: read, create and attach blobs.
///
/// `0.3.0` is this interface's version, not a third component contract. A
/// component that imports `blobs` is a `brenn:processor@0.2.0` component: it
/// is told apart by `bus@0.2.0`, which it imports because `blobs` uses
/// `bus.body`, and everything `processor-0.2.wit` and `processor.wit` say of
/// it holds. Read the three files together, `processor.wit` first, and name
/// the world `brenn:processor/processor@0.3.0`, which is the 0.2.0 world with
/// `blobs` imported.
///
/// Served by the backend host in full. A surface host serves `limits`,
/// `open`, the `blob` resource, `create`, the `writer` resource and
/// `publish`, and refuses a component that imports `call-async`,
/// `call-async-for` or `complete`.
///
/// STABLE. No type or function of `blobs@0.3.0` is changed or removed, no
/// case is added to a variant or an enum and no field to a record, and each
/// keeps doing what this file says it does. While brenn serves this
/// interface, an artifact built against the shapes in this file loads and
/// means what it meant. brenn does not stop serving it without a removal
/// period, announced in `CHANGELOG.md` and in this file no later than the
/// first release the period counts; no length is promised. A breaking change
/// is a new version of this interface, served beside this one through that
/// period. An addition is a function no existing component imports, added
/// beside the functions this interface has, which keep their shapes and
/// their behaviour; a host coming to serve a function it refuses today is an
/// addition of the same kind. A component that does not call the new
/// function imports nothing new. Bounds are host policy and outside this
/// promise.
///
/// Who may name a blob. A component names a blob only by a `blob` handle,
/// and gets one in exactly two ways: `open`, for an attachment of a message
/// in one of this activation's input port windows, and `writer.seal`, for a
/// blob it made in this activation. No function takes a blob id. An id in a
/// header, a body or a component's own store opens nothing.
///
/// Lifetime. A handle is a hold: a blob with an open handle is not deleted
/// under it. Nothing a component holds outlives the activation. On `ok` the
/// messages it published hold the blobs they name, as every message does;
/// every other handle and writer is dropped, so a sealed blob no published
/// message names and an unsealed writer's bytes are deleted. On `err` or a
/// trap nothing is published, and every blob the activation made is deleted.
/// A blob it only opened lives on by the messages that name it. The one
/// exception is `complete`: a completion holds the blobs it names from the
/// call, so a blob the activation made and completed with survives the
/// activation's `err` or trap, as the completion does.
/// On a surface a handle to an attachment of a wire message is a place in
/// the activation's windows, not a hold on the backend's blob: the page takes
/// the hold when it forwards the blob, at the flush, and a message that left
/// the server's window in between loses the flush. A page-local message holds
/// a blob the page made from the flush. A wire-bound message exists only once
/// the server applies the activation's batch; until then the page holds the
/// bytes it made, and the message may never exist (see `publish`). So "on
/// `ok` the messages it published hold the blobs they name" is true on a
/// surface of page-local messages, and of a wire-bound message if and when
/// the server applies its batch.
///
/// Bounds. The largest read and write, how many handles an activation may
/// hold, how many calls it may make and how many bytes it may write are host
/// policy, not part of any promise; `limits` reports the two per-call sizes.
/// The refusal a bound draws keeps its variant and its meaning. On the
/// backend host the bytes one activation may write come to 64 MiB and a
/// writer does not outlive its activation, so a component cannot make a
/// larger blob; that is host policy like every bound here, not a promise of
/// the interface. A surface page makes no blob larger than the smaller of
/// its own bound for the bytes it has made and the largest blob its server
/// accepts, and has no ceiling on blob calls: there `quota-exceeded` is the
/// handle bound at `create` and `open`, the bytes-written bound at `write`,
/// and never `seal`'s answer. A page has no filesystem and never answers
/// `storage-failed`.
///
/// What the other functions do with attachments. `ports.publish`,
/// `publish-with-urgency` and `publish-deferred` publish a message that
/// carries no attachments: republishing a body does not forward what the
/// original carried. `ports.defer-edit` replaces a parked message's body and
/// time and keeps its attachments; `ports.defer-cancel` releases them with
/// the message. The deferred window shows a parked message's body, index and
/// release time and not its attachments, and no function of this interface
/// reads or edits them. TODO(blobs-parked-message-attachments)
/// `tools.call-async`, `provides.call-async-for` and `provides.complete`
/// carry no attachments; `call-async`, `call-async-for` and `complete` of
/// this interface are the forms that do. A request on `tool-requests` carries
/// the attachments its caller gave, and a result on `tool-results` those the
/// tool answered with; their bodies are those of the plain forms.
/// A `receive` reply, `calls.call` and `tools.call-fast` carry no
/// attachments. TODO(blobs-on-sync-calls)
///
/// Mutability. A sealed blob is immutable, and a handle to one is proof that
/// nothing changes under its reader. The interface does not entrench that:
/// an in-place mutable blob would add a mutability flag at `create`, a write
/// path on a handle to a sealed mutable blob, and a way for a reader to learn
/// which kind it holds, so a blob without the flag keeps its guarantee. Three
/// choices lean on immutability and none forecloses mutation: `blob` has
/// `read` and no `write`, which makes a handle that proof; `size` and
/// `content-type` are fixed on a handle, so no call fails and a guest sizes
/// its reads once, where a mutable blob needs a fixed extent or a size "at
/// open"; and forwarding shares the blob rather than copying it, which is
/// zero copy, and for a mutable blob would show every holder every write.

interface blobs {
    use brenn:processor/bus@0.2.0.{body};
    use brenn:processor/ports@0.2.0.{publish-error as ports-publish-error};
    use brenn:processor/tools@0.1.0.{tool-error};
    use brenn:processor/provides@0.1.0.{complete-error as plain-complete-error};

    /// The largest single read and single write the host serving the call
    /// accepts, in bytes.
    record blob-limits {
        max-read-bytes: u32,
        max-write-bytes: u32,
    }

    /// This host's per-call bounds, so a guest chunks without learning them
    /// by failing.
    limits: func() -> blob-limits;

    variant open-error {
        /// `(port, index, name)` names no attachment of a message in this
        /// activation's input port windows: an unknown port, an index past
        /// the window, or a name the message does not carry.
        not-permitted,
        /// The message names the blob, and nothing holds it any longer: its
        /// last holder let go after the activation's windows were read.
        not-found,
        /// The activation holds as many handles as the host allows, or has
        /// made as many blob calls as it allows.
        quota-exceeded,
    }

    variant read-error {
        /// `offset + len` passes the blob's size. Never a short read.
        out-of-range,
        /// `len` is over `limits().max-read-bytes`.
        too-large,
        /// The host's filesystem refused the read. The string is a host
        /// diagnostic, opaque to the component.
        storage-failed(string),
        /// The activation has made as many blob calls as the host allows.
        quota-exceeded,
        /// The host serving the call does not hold these bytes and cannot
        /// fetch them inside the call. The backend host never answers this.
        /// A surface page answers it for an attachment it did not fetch: one
        /// larger than its channel's fetch bound, one whose fetch was refused
        /// or failed, one whose bytes the page dropped for room, or one still
        /// on its way. A later activation may find the bytes. It also answers
        /// it for a backend blob a page-local message names when the page
        /// does not hold that blob's bytes for the wire message that
        /// delivered it.
        unavailable,
    }

    variant create-error {
        /// Not a well-formed media type of at most 255 bytes, as a body's.
        invalid-content-type(string),
        /// The host serving the call is over its budget for blob bytes. On
        /// a surface that is the page's own budget for the bytes it has
        /// made; the backend store's budget is never answered in a call.
        budget-exhausted,
        /// The host's filesystem refused to create the blob. Nothing was
        /// created. The string is a host diagnostic.
        storage-failed(string),
        /// The activation holds as many handles as the host allows, or has
        /// made as many blob calls as it allows.
        quota-exceeded,
    }

    variant write-error {
        /// `bytes` is longer than `limits().max-write-bytes`.
        too-large,
        /// The write would take the blob past the largest blob the host
        /// serving the call accepts.
        blob-too-large,
        /// The write would take the host serving the call past its budget
        /// for blob bytes. On a surface that is the page's own budget for
        /// the bytes it has made.
        budget-exhausted,
        /// The write would leave the blob with more disjoint written ranges
        /// than the host tracks.
        too-fragmented,
        /// The host's filesystem refused the write. The range counts as
        /// unwritten and the writer stays usable. The string is a host
        /// diagnostic.
        storage-failed(string),
        /// The activation has made as many blob calls, or written as many
        /// bytes, as the host allows.
        quota-exceeded,
    }

    /// A range of a blob no write has covered.
    record gap {
        offset: u64,
        len: u64,
    }

    variant seal-error {
        /// The first range below the highest byte written that no write
        /// covered. The writer stays open, to be filled and sealed again.
        gap(gap),
        /// The activation has made as many blob calls as the host allows.
        quota-exceeded,
    }

    /// A sealed, immutable blob this activation holds.
    resource blob {
        /// The blob's size in bytes.
        size: func() -> u64;
        /// The content type the blob was created with.
        content-type: func() -> string;
        /// Exactly the bytes `[offset, offset + len)`. A zero-length read
        /// inside the blob is empty.
        read: func(offset: u64, len: u32) -> result<list<u8>, read-error>;
    }

    /// A handle to the attachment `name` of the message at `index` in input
    /// port `port`'s window, as this activation delivered it. Takes a hold
    /// and reads no byte, so forwarding an attachment copies nothing.
    ///
    /// On a surface it succeeds for an attachment whose bytes the page does
    /// not hold, and `not-found` is the server's answer that the message left
    /// the channel's window. On a page-local channel it opens what the
    /// message's attachment names, bytes the page made or a backend blob the
    /// page forwarded there; `not-found` there means the message left the
    /// page's window after the activation's windows were read.
    open: func(port: string, index: u32, name: string) -> result<blob, open-error>;

    /// A blob being written. Dropped unsealed, its bytes are deleted.
    resource writer {
        /// Write `bytes` at `offset`, in any order, over bytes already
        /// written. A refused write changes nothing.
        write: func(offset: u64, bytes: list<u8>) -> result<_, write-error>;
        /// Seal the blob when every byte below the highest written is
        /// covered. On success the writer is spent: a further `write` or
        /// `seal` on it traps. An empty blob seals.
        seal: func() -> result<blob, seal-error>;
    }

    /// Start a blob with `content-type`.
    create: func(content-type: string) -> result<writer, create-error>;

    /// Message urgency, as `ports.urgency`.
    enum urgency { very-low, low, normal, high }

    record publish-options {
        /// `none` is the port's configured default.
        urgency: option<urgency>,
        /// Hold the message until this time, epoch milliseconds UTC, as
        /// `ports.publish-deferred`; `none` publishes at the flush.
        deliver-after: option<u64>,
    }

    variant publish-error {
        /// A refusal `ports.publish` gives, with its meaning. `not-permitted`
        /// is also the answer to a component without the `ports` grant. For
        /// `invalid-payload` the body alone is weighed, never the
        /// attachments; for `text-only-channel` the body is judged, and a
        /// text body may carry attachments of any content type.
        plain(ports-publish-error),
        /// An attachment name outside the grammar: 1 to 64 bytes, the first
        /// ASCII alphanumeric, the rest ASCII alphanumeric or `.`, `_`, `-`.
        invalid-attachment-name(string),
        /// The same name given twice.
        duplicate-attachment-name(string),
        /// More than 32 attachments.
        too-many-attachments,
    }

    /// Buffer one message for the channel bound to `port`, carrying `body`
    /// and each named blob. Buffering, the atomic flush and the answer to
    /// `port` are those of `ports.publish`, and the message keeps its place
    /// in call order among the `ports` publishes. Publishing needs the
    /// `ports` grant; an artifact that calls this function imports
    /// `brenn:processor/ports@0.2.0` for its error type, so its class
    /// requires `ports`. The same blob may be named twice. An empty list
    /// behaves as `ports.publish`. The message holds its blobs from this
    /// call; the component may drop its handles.
    ///
    /// On a surface no attachment is refused for where its blob came from or
    /// which kind of channel its message goes to. What the page can judge it
    /// answers in the call; a wire-bound message whose upload or forward the
    /// server refuses after the activation returned `ok` is lost with the
    /// rest of the activation's wire batch, never published without its
    /// attachments, and the page announces the loss.
    ///
    /// The checks run in a fixed order: the call budget shared with `ports`;
    /// the `ports` grant; the port (undeclared traps); the output ACL; the
    /// body (`invalid-body`, then `text-only-channel`); the attachments
    /// (`too-many-attachments`, `invalid-attachment-name`,
    /// `duplicate-attachment-name`); then the size cap and budgets. A
    /// declared, unwired port checks everything that needs no channel and
    /// drops the message. Of the attachments, the count is judged first and
    /// then each name in list order, its grammar before whether an earlier
    /// name was the same, so the first name at fault decides. Every function
    /// here that takes attachments judges them so.
    publish: func(port: string, body: body,
        attachments: list<tuple<string, borrow<blob>>>,
        options: publish-options) -> result<_, publish-error>;

    /// Why a tool call that carries attachments was refused.
    variant call-error {
        /// A refusal the plain form gives, with its meaning.
        plain(tool-error),
        /// As `publish-error.invalid-attachment-name`.
        invalid-attachment-name(string),
        /// The same name given twice.
        duplicate-attachment-name(string),
        /// More than 32 attachments.
        too-many-attachments,
        /// The tool takes no attachments. Told only to a caller granted the
        /// tool.
        attachments-not-accepted,
    }

    /// `tools.call-async`, carrying each named blob to the tool. Needs the
    /// `tools` grant: without it every call is `plain(not-granted)`. An
    /// artifact that calls it imports `brenn:processor/tools@0.1.0` for its
    /// error type, so its class requires `tools`.
    ///
    /// Buffered and flushed exactly as the plain form: the request reaches
    /// the bus iff the activation returns `ok`, and holds its blobs from this
    /// call, so the component may drop its handles. On `err` or a trap the
    /// request is dropped and its holds with it. It shares the plain form's
    /// per-activation call cap, the publish-entry ceiling, and the `call-id`
    /// and `args-json` caps. The same blob may be given under two names. An
    /// empty list behaves as the plain form.
    ///
    /// The checks run in a fixed order: the call cap; the `tools` grant; the
    /// entry ceiling; `call-id`; `args-json`; the attachments
    /// (`too-many-attachments`, `invalid-attachment-name`,
    /// `duplicate-attachment-name`); the plain form's checks (grant, class,
    /// arguments, ACL); last `attachments-not-accepted`.
    call-async: func(tool: string, args-json: string, call-id: string,
        attachments: list<tuple<string, borrow<blob>>>) -> result<_, call-error>;

    /// `provides.call-async-for`, carrying each named blob. Needs the
    /// `provides` grant, as the plain form does: without it every call is
    /// `plain(not-granted)`. The nested call is admitted by the component's
    /// own tool grants and its result arrives on `tool-results`, so a
    /// provider that makes one requires `tools` as well; an artifact that
    /// calls it imports `brenn:processor/tools@0.1.0` for its error type.
    /// Buffered, bounded and checked as `call-async`, with the `provides`
    /// grant in place of `tools`.
    call-async-for: func(serving: string, tool: string, args-json: string,
        call-id: string, attachments: list<tuple<string, borrow<blob>>>)
        -> result<_, call-error>;

    variant complete-error {
        /// The component lacks the `provides` grant.
        not-permitted,
        /// A refusal the plain form gives, with its meaning: `not-pending`,
        /// or `invalid-outcome`, which is also the answer to an `err`
        /// outcome given with attachments.
        plain(plain-complete-error),
        invalid-attachment-name(string),
        duplicate-attachment-name(string),
        too-many-attachments,
    }

    /// `provides.complete`, answering with each named blob beside the
    /// outcome. Immediate, as the plain form: the completion holds the blobs
    /// it names from this call, and a later `err` or trap of the activation
    /// retracts neither. It shares the plain form's per-activation cap, and
    /// passing it traps. A result carries attachments only when its outcome
    /// is `ok`. An artifact that calls it imports
    /// `brenn:processor/provides@0.1.0` for its error type, so its class
    /// requires `provides`.
    ///
    /// The checks run in a fixed order: the cap; the `provides` grant; the
    /// outcome text's size; the attachments; then the plain form's checks,
    /// with the refusal of an `err` outcome given attachments after the
    /// outcome parses.
    complete: func(id: string, outcome-json: string,
        attachments: list<tuple<string, borrow<blob>>>) -> result<_, complete-error>;
}

world processor {
    include brenn:processor/processor@0.2.0;
    import blobs;
}
